WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.690
CROVITZ: Good evening, everyone.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.430
I'm your presider, Gordon Crovitz.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:15.719
And welcome to the Council on Foreign Relations
meeting on privacy and security in the digital

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:17.300
age.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:23.710
We will, as is always the case, have plenty
of time for questions.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.900
So please prepare those for members.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:33.430
We're very lucky to have a panel of people
who are both highly experienced and highly

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.870
opinionated.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.710
So we should have a very good evening.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:43.940
There are bios in your materials, but Michael
Chertoff is chairman of the Chertoff Group,

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.469
formerly served as secretary of Homeland Security,
was also a prosecutor and a federal appeals

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:51.050
court judge.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:56.870
Cyrus Vance, Jr. is the district attorney
for New York, better known in the movies and

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.340
thrillers as the Manhattan DA.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.510
(Laughter.)

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:05.710
And to my right is Adam Segal, who directs
the Council's digital and cyberspace policy

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.620
program.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:15.340
So I think the biggest issue in this area
of privacy and security is what the founders

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:22.000
would have made of this-(laughter)-and all
the other smartphones in the room.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.930
The Fourth Amendment says reasonable searches
and seizures are permitted.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:33.290
What happens when technology makes them not
possible?

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:40.810
So I wanted to start by asking Cy Vance, if
you would, to talk a little bit about the

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.600
analog era.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.590
So before all of these digital issues came
up, before you had issues like in San Bernardino

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:53.369
where we have the dispute between Apple and
the FBI over whether Apple should be obliged

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:59.780
to help the FBI access that phone, remind
us what the world was like in the analog era.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.130
What kinds of information could prosecutors
get with a traditional warrant?

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:11.690
VANCE: The backdrop to this is I'm speaking
really on state and local law enforcement

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.800
issues, just so I don't appear to be a federal
expert when I'm not.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:31.090
And really up until September of 2014, we
accessed the contents of mobile devices, smartphones,

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:38.200
if we could get a warrant from a judge focused
on a specific phone.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:44.620
And the range of information that we would
get out of that phone was enormous from time

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.040
to time, and extremely helpful.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:59.250
I think it's fair to say that in the last
15 years, up until the fall of 2014, and whether

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:06.690
it was a case involving homicide or financial
crime, digital evidence, very, very often

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:14.310
on smartphones, was a key part of gathering
the evidence for a case.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:22.250
So the way the world worked up until late
September, 2014 is among the many avenues

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:28.050
we would investigate for evidence relevant
to the case would be in smartphones, as well

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.440
as laptops, and other devices.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:36.989
I just want to add that it was very important
and is very important for us in terms of building

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:44.170
cases, but I also think it's very important
in terms of making sure that prosecutors had

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:47.420
all the information they needed to make the
right calls.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:54.260
We have had cases in our office where because
of our ability to access a phone, when police

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:58.360
arrested someone for homicide and I had a
very smart assistant over the course of the

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:04.319
weekend who just didn't think that the timeline
was adding up, we could get that phone out

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:11.410
to Apple, fly it out with a warrant, bring
it back, and in this case my assistant was

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:17.810
able to cut lose the gentleman who'd been
identified and arrested for murder by the

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:24.850
police, find the true killer, who is now serving
a lengthy term in prison.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:31.220
So the point is, it was important not only
to build cases-or essential.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:36.770
It's also important to make sure that we are
exercising our responsibilities as prosecutors

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.280
to the degree that I think the court expects
us to.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:45.340
And there is going to come a time, because
of the inability to access phones which hold

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:51.580
a huge amount of evidence, that judges are
going to be dealing with motions by lawyers

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:58.150
who are saying that we believe in that phone
is information that could exculpate my client.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:03.840
And we're going to have to sort through how
we move forward in those cases.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.759
I think everybody in this room would want
the prosecutor investigating your case, or

00:05:08.759 --> 00:05:11.900
someone you know, to have all the facts.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.150
We now live in a world where we are not getting
all the facts.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:19.830
Many of the facts are on smartphones because
criminals, just like you and me, have moved

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.360
off-paper and onto digital devices.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:27.220
So that's the pre-world and the post-world
is-I'm sure we'll talk about.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:33.880
CROVITZ: And just to really level-set this
discussion, even before-going back before

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:41.669
the smartphone, prosecutors could get access
to bank security boxes, to health records,

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:46.479
all sorts of things that we in the digital
era would say are on our phones, but at that

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:47.800
time were more available.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:53.860
VANCE: Search warrants enabled us to get access
to really anything, but only with the authority

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:54.870
of a court.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:06:01.560
And, you know, the devices today are really
the only-as far as I can tell, the only warrant-proof

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.949
devices that exist.

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:10.910
And that is posing a challenge I think to
both sides.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:15.710
SEGAL: I wonder-if I can ask-because it strikes
me that, Gordon, you're getting to an important

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:20.740
point, which is that qualitatively all of
the data on the phone is different because

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:25.590
all of those things are in one place, where
on the analogy, right, you'd have to do the

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.550
legwork to get all of those specific things.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.009
VANCE: I mean, maybe, maybe not.

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:37.639
If you-when Mike was U.S. attorney he probably,
like I have, when officers go out and do search

00:06:37.639 --> 00:06:42.440
warrants, they probably have three or four
locations-office, home, associates.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.479
It wasn't-it's not that complicated.

00:06:45.479 --> 00:06:51.660
And maybe you hit 20 places with search warrants,
but still it is true that much of this information

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:57.160
is now collapsed into a single device, but
it's the same kind of information that would

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:00.849
have been accessible to a warrant, whether
it was in a car, a home, a safety deposit

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:02.680
box, or some physical location.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:09.440
CROVITZ: And last time I saw, you had estimated
that in your office there are 200 iPhones

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:14.090
or so that you would like to be able to get
access to, but have not been able to.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:20.449
VANCE: It was clear to me when Apple moved
to reengineer its phones to be incapable of

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:24.110
being opened, even with a warrant, that this
was going to have an impact on the way we

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:25.960
did business.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:29.930
We in the Manhattan DA's office created our
own cyber lab over the last several years

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:36.780
because the NYPD simply can't keep up with
the volume of devices that we're asking them

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.570
to interrogate at the speed we're asking them
to do it.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.360
So we started our own lab.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:47.340
And of the phones that have submitted to our
lab, of the 750 phones that have been submitted

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:57.379
to our lab that we have gotten-we've gotten
warrants for all the Apple devices, and 230

00:07:57.379 --> 00:08:02.199
of those devices are inaccessible to us.

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:07.090
Most at this point are iPhone 6's running
with operating systems eight and above.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:09.590
CROVITZ: So let me turn now to Michael Chertoff.

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:12.020
You've been on different sides of this issue.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.699
You've been a prosecutor, head of Homeland
Security.

00:08:15.699 --> 00:08:20.569
You've been one of the more public proponents,
I think, of the argument that there should

00:08:20.569 --> 00:08:27.250
not be any ban or tinkering with end-to-end
encryption, of the kind that Apple, and Google,

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:31.590
and others have now executed for their operating
systems.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:32.590
Explain that.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:37.181
CHERTOFF: And first, let me come back to the
way the world was prior to smartphones, because

00:08:37.181 --> 00:08:42.390
since time immemorial people have tried to
frustrate law enforcement's ability to get

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:46.000
evidence, even when there's a lawful basis
to get it.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.870
So we were talking earlier, back in the day
when I was a prosecutor and I was getting

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:55.010
wiretap or electronic surveillance warrants,
you know, that allowed us to lawfully put

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:59.700
a tap on a phone or put a recording device
in a building.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.130
It wasn't long before bad guys figured that
out, so what they would do is they would turn

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:07.390
the radio up so you couldn't have an audible,
discernible voice.

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:10.240
Or they'd communicate in sign language.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.120
Or they'd walk around the block and have a
conversation outside of earshot.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:19.399
Now, those were all frustrating the ability
of law enforcement to use a warrant to get

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:25.310
information, but we accepted that's the nature
of free society, is you can't make people

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.890
sit in a room and talk audibly.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:28.890
(Laughter.)

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:34.620
So I would argue that this is a species of
a problem we've faced on and off for many,

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:36.760
many years.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:42.330
I also think that one of the things that you
have to consider is end-to-end encryption's

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:47.530
only a way of a number of different ways people
will secure data.

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:51.620
For example, you have, you know, various kinds
of apps that will cause messages to disappear

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:53.080
after you read them.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:57.050
That's a security device, as is encryption.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:02.890
So the challenge is, to what extent, as you
balance the legitimate and understandable-and

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:08.810
I don't minimize this-desire of law enforcement
to get evidence that can be critical in a

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:14.480
case-how do you balance that against the ability
of people to secure their own data, particularly

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.880
in a world in which the government tells people
with respect to cybersecurity: Don't count

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:21.130
on us to protect you.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:22.600
You have to protect your own data.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.930
And so to me, what you see is really a tradeoff
not between security and privacy, but between

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:29.360
two types of security.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:36.640
The last thing I would say, which is relevant
to the smartphone, the volume of data is now

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.070
hugely greater.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:44.050
And one of the interesting legal issues that's
emerging now is to what extent even a warrant

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:51.750
for a phone, or an accepted warrantless legal
basis to search a phone changes when the phone

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:55.200
itself becomes a different kind of device.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:00.000
I think because it's handling so much information
that it's far beyond what in the old days

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.180
you could have considered, you know, within
a person's personal control.

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:08.490
But beyond that, because often the data's
no longer on the device itself.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:12.070
The device is literally a key to data being
stored in the cloud.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:16.050
And this comes up, for example, in the area
of border searches.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:20.800
The rule has been-and I argued for this when
I was secretary of Homeland Security-that

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.040
when you cross a border the government is
entitled to look at everything you bring with

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.040
you.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:28.820
And if it's a briefcase, they can look in
the briefcase.

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:34.850
And the law was, if it was a laptop, anything
on the laptop could be looked at because you're

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:37.200
bringing in what might be contraband.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:43.970
But now I ask myself, does that change when
in effect the phone is no longer the repository

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:48.930
of the data, but is the key to get the data
that's already in the cloud.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:52.269
And that would be a little bit, to my mind,
like if I crossed the border with my house

00:11:52.269 --> 00:11:56.649
key and someone said, OK, now I want to have
your key and then go to your house and look

00:11:56.649 --> 00:11:57.790
at everything in your house.

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:00.089
I don't think we would consider that a border
search.

00:12:00.089 --> 00:12:08.610
So in many ways, the technology is confounding
even the preexisting legal categories.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:15.180
CROVITZ: You co-wrote an op-ed in The Washington
Post about a year go on this topic that I

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:16.570
thought had a fascinating conclusion.

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:17.970
I've been curious what you meant.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.810
And this is my big chance to ask you, so I'm
going to.

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:21.810
(Laughter.)

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:22.810
I'm going to remind you by quoting you.

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:28.290
What you wrote was: If law enforcement and
intelligence organizations face a future without

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:34.850
assured access to encrypted communications,
they will develop technologies and techniques

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:36.839
to meet their legitimate mission goals.

00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:43.589
And of course, in the case later, after you
wrote this, the FBI did get from a third party

00:12:43.589 --> 00:12:48.589
a means for accessing the San Bernardino terrorist's
phone.

00:12:48.589 --> 00:12:57.220
Were you suggesting that law enforcement and
intelligence agencies won't go dark, as the

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:58.940
FBI director has warned?

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:00.720
CHERTOFF: I was saying two things.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:07.279
One is, I think it's actually the nature of
encryption that it's never clear to me that

00:13:07.279 --> 00:13:09.860
you reach a stage where you have perfect encryption.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:13.980
If you know anything about public and private
key encryption, the essence of creating the

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:20.010
private key is to have very long sequences
of prime numbers that would require years

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.610
of constant computing to break.

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:28.459
But actually I was-today I was shown a chart
about various contests that have been run

00:13:28.459 --> 00:13:35.790
over the last few years to see whether people
could break ever longer encrypted keys.

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:40.100
And not surprisingly, over time people have
found ways to break them.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:45.579
Now, there will always be another key that's
even longer-although there'll be some constraints

00:13:45.579 --> 00:13:49.639
on the ability to do that because the ability
then to manage the data itself will become

00:13:49.639 --> 00:13:51.970
a little bit challenging.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:57.579
But my point is, it's not in the nature of
encryption that it is a static issue.

00:13:57.579 --> 00:14:00.459
There will always be new innovations.

00:14:00.459 --> 00:14:01.940
And there will also be new countermeasures.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:06.589
And I think we saw that in the '90s when there
was a debate about whether there needed to

00:14:06.589 --> 00:14:12.769
be-was there going to be concerns about telephonic
encryption and whether there needed to be

00:14:12.769 --> 00:14:15.720
a requirement that that be limited as well.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:23.269
The second point I was making was that there's
no vastly more information available-apart

00:14:23.269 --> 00:14:28.200
from the encrypted content itself-than we
had even 10, 15 years ago.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:34.420
I mean, this is a paradise for intelligence
agents and law enforcement agents because

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:41.360
of the amount of data that's generated from
unencrypted locational data, metadata, financial

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:45.399
data, et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:45.399 --> 00:14:50.980
And in terms of what is the intelligence challenge
of locating people who are potential threats,

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:56.690
which is finding the needle in the haystack,
the haystack is now so large that it's the

00:14:56.690 --> 00:15:00.240
kind of the thing we only fantasized about
15 years ago after 9/11.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:06.540
So again, without minimizing the challenges-particularly
if you're going to present a case in court-that

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:12.949
are posed by encryption, I think encryption
is not by its nature ever in a permanent state

00:15:12.949 --> 00:15:15.540
of being perfect.

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:19.650
And second, there are so many other kinds
of information and evidence out there that

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:27.300
net, if I'm an investigator, I'd much rather
be where I am in 2015 than where I was, let's

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.580
say, in 2010 or 2005.

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:36.639
CROVITZ: So, Adam, in your new book, "The
Hacked World Order," you made the point that

00:15:36.639 --> 00:15:41.550
as we discuss these issues in the U.S., we're
really not discussing them in a vacuum.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:45.600
And we'll talk in a moment about potential
legislation.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:52.029
But describe what you meant by that, that
these kinds of issues are-may be in that way

00:15:52.029 --> 00:15:58.579
a little bit more complicated than a generation
ago when we had telecom regulations that applied

00:15:58.579 --> 00:15:59.949
to domestic telecom companies.

00:15:59.949 --> 00:16:06.170
SEGAL: Well, I think first is the reality
that the U.S. has no monopoly on the technology,

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:07.170
right?

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:11.240
Even if we were to come up to some discussion
that we were going to ban certain types of

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:15.290
encryption, it will be developed other places,
right?

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:18.850
So I think that is a huge problem.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:22.949
The second is clearly there's a huge demonstration
effect going on.

00:16:22.949 --> 00:16:28.800
We can see that the Chinese and others have
looked at the debate.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:37.449
You know, the Indians introduced and then
very quickly removed a ban on encryption when

00:16:37.449 --> 00:16:41.079
the realized that everyone was going to have
to hold onto their plaintext for years, and

00:16:41.079 --> 00:16:43.440
they decided that it was not manageable.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:49.040
So there's clearly a kind of demonstration
effect that's going on to other countries.

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:56.790
And then, you know, there is the larger issue
of what we want-what the goals are for U.S.

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:57.889
technology companies, right?

00:16:57.889 --> 00:17:02.920
So what are-what are we thinking about their
competitiveness and how they're going to be

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:04.579
in other markets.

00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:10.210
You know, the FBI director, of course, had
kind of thrown it off and said that this is

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:16.870
a marketing strategy, but we saw, you know,
last week when a Brazilian judge knocked WhatsApp

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:23.850
offline for about a day and a half, and several
millions-tens of millions of Brazilian users

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:29.880
didn't get access to it, that all of these
interactions are closely tied to security

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:30.980
and economics.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:34.970
CROVITZ: So, Cy Vance, I wanted to ask you
a question along these lines.

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:42.450
We've heard good arguments about the complications
of potential regulation, and the technology

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.230
is complex.

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:52.780
On the other hand, Apple and Google, in a
way, created a change in the situation by

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:55.290
moving to default encryption.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:18:05.270
So before they did this in 2014, were there
reasons for them to be concerned about hacking?

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:13.669
VANCE: This is what has puzzled me personally
about the communication on this issue after

00:18:13.669 --> 00:18:19.100
Apple moved to default device encryption,
September 2014.

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:24.230
At that time, Apple was operating on iOS 7.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:26.180
We are now on iOS 9.

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:29.690
And it was I think before the six came out.

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:35.240
Now, in that time period, this was the time
period where we were presenting affidavits

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:37.500
and warrants to judges for them to review.

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:42.890
And if they approved it, we took them to Apple,
and Apple did whatever magic it did, and returned

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:45.650
to us the responsive material.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:54.320
There was no indication that Apple's ability
to maintain the security of the phone content

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:55.320
was an issue.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:19:01.450
In fact, Apple said exactly the opposite,
that their iOS 7 was state of the art.

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:06.190
And also, interestingly, said they felt they
had a responsibility as a corporation to make

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:10.110
sure that law enforcement was able to get
the information it needed, to find the Alzheimer's

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:14.770
patient or other issues on an emergent basis.

00:19:14.770 --> 00:19:20.600
So then default device encryption came in
in September '14.

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:29.150
So I think the question that Apple needs to
answer, from my position, is has there ever

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:39.419
been a situation where when a law enforcement
agency sends a phone out to Apple to be decrypted

00:19:39.419 --> 00:19:41.720
there was ever a compromise of data on that
phone.

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:46.600
I mean, that's really the fundamental thing
that started all this was the concern about

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:53.250
what is called a backdoor, which I disagree
with, and that somehow the world's going to

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.360
end.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:20:03.570
But I've asked this question in letters to
Apple and Google in March of 2015.

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:07.380
I'm still waiting for the courtesy of a response.

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:12.940
And I think we need-if you're going to-if
we are going to measure, as we have to, what

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:19.910
is the risk of maintaining the ability to
open a phone by the company, what is the added

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:29.840
risk that that puts us in harm's way of, versus
what is the consequence to law enforcement

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.040
of not being able to access those phones.

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:38.650
I can tell you I have 230 cases in my office
from homicide to sex abuse to child abuse

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:43.870
to drugs to guns that I can't get into those
phones.

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:50.000
What is Apple telling me about how many times
a phone that was delivered by a law enforcement

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:55.990
agency to Apple as a result of their unlocking
the phone pursuant to court order that data

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:57.480
was ever compromised?

00:20:57.480 --> 00:20:59.990
So I think we need an answer.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:03.390
CHERTOFF: So I think, Gordon, there are two
types of encryption, and we have to be careful

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:05.809
not to confuse them.

00:21:05.809 --> 00:21:11.140
The more general issue has to do with end-to-end
encryption, which has to do with when you're

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:17.200
either dealing with data in motion or data
at rest you encrypt it, except at the point

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:20.040
of initiation and the point of receipt.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:25.250
And obviously there that's a critical element
in securing against people hacking into a

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:30.590
network and stealing your data, and relevant
to financial institutions, health care institutions,

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:31.590
et cetera.

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:36.471
Now, the issue that Cy is talking about is
a somewhat narrower issue that has to do deal

00:21:36.471 --> 00:21:38.659
with a feature of the phone.

00:21:38.659 --> 00:21:40.480
The encryption itself is very simple.

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:42.860
You're talking about four digits.

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:48.740
But it's designed to defeat efforts to repetitively
try different variations by slowing up the

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:54.010
time between each entry and ultimately stopping
anything further on the phone and wiping it

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:56.740
if you do it more than a certain number of
times.

00:21:56.740 --> 00:22:01.660
That's really designed to deal with the problem
of somebody stealing your phone and then if

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:06.740
they didn't have that feature they could infinitely
go and try, you know, a number of combinations

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:09.860
and crack the phone, you know, relatively
easily.

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:14.120
The issue in Apple, which is technical-and
I should just disclose, we do work with Apple,

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:21.549
so it's out there-was whether creating a tool
that would allow you to basically substitute

00:22:21.549 --> 00:22:27.740
out the operating system that currently exists
for a system that did not have that feature-namely

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:32.200
the one that shuts down the phone after you
have too many tries-whether that once it was

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:37.650
created would potentially get released and
compromise other phones.

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:42.830
At the end of the day, in a way, this is lawyers
call a moot point, because inevitably what

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:46.950
you're going to have going forward is a new
system that's configured in such a way so

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:51.559
as to be even more protective of the device
itself.

00:22:51.559 --> 00:22:56.409
And if the U.S. government were to say it's
illegal to do that, I guarantee you, you would

00:22:56.409 --> 00:23:00.840
be able to buy a phone that does that within
10 minutes on the dark web.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:09.120
So again, I think in some cases, on the narrower
issue, the lifespan of what we've been fighting

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:10.610
about is very short.

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:15.710
The broader issue however is very, very important,
which is do we preserve the right to encrypt

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:19.480
data at rest or in motion within a network
and on the Internet.

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:24.929
VANCE: I got to disagree with my colleague,
because I don't think that's-I think that's

00:23:24.929 --> 00:23:28.929
a framing of the issue, but I don't think
that's how it was framed for us by Apple.

00:23:28.929 --> 00:23:36.360
Tim Cook said that were there to be, in his
words, a backdoor, which he means Apple maintaining

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:44.559
a key to open its device, he alluded it to
threatening millions of Apple consumers.

00:23:44.559 --> 00:23:48.710
He likened it to causing cancer.

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:56.100
So it was very much about-if Apple maintains
a digital key, as it did up until September

00:23:56.100 --> 00:24:06.610
2014, is that such a risk that we should forego
the ability to access those phones in the

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:12.010
service of causing cases and-cases for victims.

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:15.049
And for what increased level of security?

00:24:15.049 --> 00:24:19.310
I simply don't think Apple has, as I indicated,
answered the question.

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:22.770
And, yes, there are many aspects of data in
transit.

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:24.670
That's very important.

00:24:24.670 --> 00:24:30.309
But in terms of just accessing a phone, I'm
just not aware that there's ever been-

00:24:30.309 --> 00:24:34.740
SEGAL: I think perhaps the answer is out there
in the sense that by 2014, or certainly if

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:39.309
not by now, there is nobody who can say we
can defend ourselves.

00:24:39.309 --> 00:24:45.370
So even if we don't know that Apple has ever
been breached and that this tool has been

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:51.190
stolen, this general sense would be nobody
could ever say we can protect whatever it

00:24:51.190 --> 00:24:52.190
is we created.

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:54.980
VANCE: But you have to measure-you have to
quantify it.

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:59.530
If you are going to say: We are taking a whole
body of evidence that is essential-that has

00:24:59.530 --> 00:25:04.990
been essential for centuries, use of warrants
based upon probable cause, reasonable searches,

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:10.180
to get evidence to build cases against people
committing crimes, you have to quantify the

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:15.279
risk that results from this ability to unlock
your phone.

00:25:15.279 --> 00:25:17.880
It's never been done.

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:18.960
Did it happen once?

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.320
Did it happen a thousand times?

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:22.419
Has it happened a hundred thousand times?

00:25:22.419 --> 00:25:25.620
Right now, that number has never been put
on the table.

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:31.100
And until we quantify that number, you're
asking the entire United States, all of law

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:34.529
enforcement and all the public, to say it's
a crisis.

00:25:34.529 --> 00:25:39.531
SEGAL: Well, plus the risk that Secretary
Chertoff said, that now with the golden age

00:25:39.531 --> 00:25:42.620
of surveillance there are other ways of perhaps
breaking those keys.

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:44.240
So-
VANCE: Well, that's not a risk, that's-

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:45.530
SEGAL: Well, that's a mitigating factor.

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:50.169
So-
VANCE: You know, I've never met a victim yet-a

00:25:50.169 --> 00:25:54.860
mother of a daughter who has been sexually
assaulted, a parent of a child that's been

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:59.900
murdered, a police officer investigating a
terrorisms case-who said to me, oh, I don't

00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:01.990
really care if you get the conversation.

00:26:01.990 --> 00:26:04.799
I just want to know the metadata.

00:26:04.799 --> 00:26:07.740
Juries expect proof beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:13.290
And the content of communications is what
proves intent in a criminal case.

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:19.440
And it's not just being able to say that Cy
Vance emailed Mike Chertoff on Saturday morning.

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:21.480
That will get you so far.

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:29.110
But I'm pretty clear that that doesn't get
you where you-if it was personal to you, you

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:31.429
would want the government to be able to build
the evidence.

00:26:31.429 --> 00:26:33.210
CHERTOFF: Yeah, so I guess I would say this.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:38.150
Again, I totally understand that if your job
is to be a prosecutor you want every bit of

00:26:38.150 --> 00:26:39.860
evidence you can get.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:43.490
And that's why prosecutors don't want to suppress
evidence, have evidence suppressed when it's

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:45.780
gotten illegally, for example.

00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:50.270
But let me tell you, I mean-use the example
I talked about earlier.

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:55.800
In the pre-smartphone days, when we had electronic
surveillance, and I did organized crime cases.

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:58.570
And the guys I investigated did not talk.

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:02.039
They used sign language or they walked around
the block.

00:27:02.039 --> 00:27:06.520
And I remember convicting people of murder,
and I didn't have conversations.

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:11.710
What I did have was pictures of people walking
around the block-(laughter)-and records of

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:16.970
telephone call from point A to point B. And
somehow, you know, we were able to fumble

00:27:16.970 --> 00:27:19.950
our way to getting those folks sentenced to
a hundred years in prison.

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:25.890
So I don't want to minimize the importance,
but to me it's not an all or nothing proposition

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:26.890
how you can do that.

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:31.140
VANCE: And those cases were likely built on
informants who took the stand and said: I

00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:32.140
talked to Mickey.

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:35.340
Mickey told me to kill-
CHERTOFF: Actually not, but I won't bore you

00:27:35.340 --> 00:27:36.600
with war stories.

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:37.600
(Laughter.)

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:40.830
CROVITZ: So we're about to go to the question
period.

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:42.779
And please wait for a microphone.

00:27:42.779 --> 00:27:47.230
And the usual Council rules-stand up, identify
yourself, be brief in your question.

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:52.990
But before we get to questions, I just want
to take the presider's right here to observe

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:59.510
that the Democratic Party member represents
the law and order side of this panel.

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:00.510
(Laughter.)

00:28:00.510 --> 00:28:06.340
Michael Chertoff, which has worked with Republican
administrations is opposing law enforcement's

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:07.340
request.

00:28:07.340 --> 00:28:08.340
(Laughter.)

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:12.390
And yet another example for those of us from
New York that Washington is becoming increasingly

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:13.390
unpredictable.

00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:14.390
(Laughter.)

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:20.390
CHERTOFF: Gordon, I-in all seriousness, Gordon,
I do want to make a point, which is to me

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:24.630
this is actually because a choice between
two types of security.

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:29.450
And I do think that the issue of being able
to have, particularly I'm not talking about

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:37.320
so much device encryption as end-to-end network
encryption, is really-like someone like all

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:43.020
you who transacts with banks online or does
other personal things online, or even has

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:47.450
personal emails that are sensitive that you
don't want to have out there-the ability to

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:51.429
have those encrypted is part of securing what's
valuable to me.

00:28:51.429 --> 00:28:55.590
And since the government doesn't take the
responsibility, and shouldn't, of operating

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:58.370
and owning everything on my network, it's
on me to do it.

00:28:58.370 --> 00:29:01.679
And all I want is the tools to carry it out.

00:29:01.679 --> 00:29:07.200
CROVITZ: I think the first question is in
the front row.

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:08.880
Q: Amitai Etzioni, George Washington.

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:11.140
I wonder-
VANCE: I don't think your mic is on.

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:12.320
Q: OK.

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:13.500
Thank you.

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:18.450
I wonder if there's a whole other level of
discussion which I would love to learn about,

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:23.360
if these things are being decided on a completely
different level kind of politically.

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:27.590
People are afraid to touch Google and Apple.

00:29:27.590 --> 00:29:29.710
They have strong lobbying.

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:33.149
They have libertarian ideologies to support
them.

00:29:33.149 --> 00:29:38.540
So all these intellectual arguments, which
I cherish-and that's the way I spend my life-are

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:40.919
really not going to decide this issue.

00:29:40.919 --> 00:29:46.850
And therefore, we hear these arguments which
don't stand a minimal examination-for instance,

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:52.029
the notion that if Americans cannot get Apple
phones they're going to take Chinese phones

00:29:52.029 --> 00:29:58.610
and use them, and trust China not to have
a backdoor; or it doesn't matter if you're

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:02.909
going to be out of business for five years,
dark, because five years from now somebody

00:30:02.909 --> 00:30:05.029
else will come up with a way to break encryptions.

00:30:05.029 --> 00:30:07.410
None of these arguments hold a minimal exam.

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:10.040
But isn't that really not what's happening
here?

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:14.570
Isn't it just a political struggle, including
the fact the president was very reluctant

00:30:14.570 --> 00:30:16.060
to speak to this issue?

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:19.470
CROVITZ: Who would like to take this question?

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:22.070
VANCE: Well, I'll just jump in.

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:24.570
I don't think it's just a political issue.

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:26.820
And I'm just speaking about myself personally.

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:33.409
You know, I am elected to be the head law
enforcement elected official for New York,

00:30:33.409 --> 00:30:37.029
which is the number one-New York City-number
one terrorism target in the country.

00:30:37.029 --> 00:30:39.750
I have some of the most serious crimes occurring
in my jurisdiction.

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:44.740
So this is not-neither academic nor political,
it's very practical.

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:49.000
And for folks like me around the country,
95 percent of the cases-criminal cases in

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:53.630
the country don't occur in federal courts,
they occur in state courts.

00:30:53.630 --> 00:31:00.380
And so the biggest impact of inability to
access at the very least the content of the

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:06.399
devices themselves, putting aside for the
moment the data in transit, matters greatly.

00:31:06.399 --> 00:31:12.740
And of course, what we're doing is talking
to political leaders, Senators and others,

00:31:12.740 --> 00:31:17.130
to try to convince them, in my view, that
they should address this with federal legislation.

00:31:17.130 --> 00:31:20.200
I think that has to be the solution.

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:24.260
And they need to do it quickly because I have
a-and I think we should all have-a sense of

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:29.669
urgency around this issue not being resolved.

00:31:29.669 --> 00:31:36.000
So, respectfully, politics doesn't enter into
this for me at all.

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:41.080
And I think I represent a large percentage
of the law enforcement community that is trying

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:42.390
to wrestle with this.

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:46.841
CROVITZ: Just to rephrase the question a little
bit, and try to elicit another response, when

00:31:46.841 --> 00:31:53.529
Apple announced its change to its operating
system in 2014, it posted on its website this

00:31:53.529 --> 00:32:01.230
line, that said, it's not-it's no longer technically
feasible for us to respond to government warrants,

00:32:01.230 --> 00:32:07.899
which I think may be one of the examples that
the FBI director used to say they were marketing

00:32:07.899 --> 00:32:10.820
their inability even to respond to government
warrants.

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:14.220
So-
VANCE: Gordon, another screen had that same

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:21.039
phrase when they just-they came out with iOS
7 and said: Apple, unlike its competitors,

00:32:21.039 --> 00:32:22.470
cannot open its devices.

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:24.690
So I think-
SEGAL: And we haven't said the word "Snowden"

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:25.690
yet.

00:32:25.690 --> 00:32:31.019
I mean, I think we do have to realize that
there was a motivation from the companies

00:32:31.019 --> 00:32:35.890
to create distance between them and the U.S.
government and all other governments.

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:42.029
So you know, I do think there was that framing
that was out there that we just can't ignore.

00:32:42.029 --> 00:32:44.840
CROVITZ: In the back.

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:51.720
Q: Alan Raul, Sidley Austin, and former vice
chair of the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:52.720
Board.

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:59.570
So your reframing of the prior question and
Adam's response really goes to my question,

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:05.029
which is: Would you address the kind of background
context of what the U.S. government can do

00:33:05.029 --> 00:33:10.919
to address the concerns that were inflamed
by Snowden, and the sense of overreach in

00:33:10.919 --> 00:33:16.810
the acquisition of data by the U.S. government,
which I don't think is a fair perception,

00:33:16.810 --> 00:33:18.980
but it is the context?

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:26.230
And this panel was titled privacy and security,
and this debate.

00:33:26.230 --> 00:33:31.720
And the privacy side is this perception that
too much data is being acquired by the U.S.

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:33.769
government, without justification.

00:33:33.769 --> 00:33:39.399
Again, unfair, but without an effective rebuttal
by the Federal government, you know, by city

00:33:39.399 --> 00:33:41.060
and state governments.

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:45.039
So what can be done to address that?

00:33:45.039 --> 00:33:49.149
And perhaps, you know, the government needs
to look at some of the cases that it brings.

00:33:49.149 --> 00:33:53.950
You know, there's pending before the 2nd Circuit
now the extraterritorial search warrant case,

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:54.950
right?

00:33:54.950 --> 00:34:01.269
So that is an example, perhaps, of the U.S.
government overreaching into servers in other

00:34:01.269 --> 00:34:08.129
countries that, again, gives rise to this
dynamic of creating distance between the companies

00:34:08.129 --> 00:34:09.190
and the government.

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:13.330
And I think if you would address, what can
the government do to address that perhaps,

00:34:13.330 --> 00:34:15.409
you know, mitigate the tension?

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:16.909
CHERTOFF: So let me take this first.

00:34:16.909 --> 00:34:21.050
And you know, Alan here has two separate issues.

00:34:21.050 --> 00:34:25.630
On the issue-the first issue you raised, I
think that really is what they call the 215

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:27.190
issue, or the metadata issue.

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:32.481
There is-and first of all, I mean, I have
nothing good to say about Snowden, nor do

00:34:32.481 --> 00:34:38.179
I credit his version of what he put out there
as sort of being accurate.

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:43.060
But the sense got out there that government-because
the government collected and stored a lot

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:49.450
of metadata, which is literally phone number,
IP address, originating communication, the

00:34:49.450 --> 00:34:53.379
address to which it went, and duration-not
even a name.

00:34:53.379 --> 00:34:57.760
Which is kept so you can look for patterns,
if you have a predicate to do so.

00:34:57.760 --> 00:35:03.290
You know, that treated as somehow a gross
violation of the law.

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:09.380
Now, the first answer is, when the government
finally belated declassified much of their

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:15.400
legal foundation for this, including opinions
written by the FISA Court, it became clear

00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:19.910
that in fact this was comfortably within the
law, it didn't go outside the law.

00:35:19.910 --> 00:35:24.230
And beyond that, that it was really minimal
intrusion into privacy.

00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:30.700
And was essentially what a telephone billing
record has in the modern age.

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:35.840
And I think there was a failure to-a disappointing
failure on the part of the government to explain,

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:38.400
in fact, that this was pretty modest.

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:41.010
And there's enormous value to be gained by
doing this.

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:45.900
And I say this as somehow who in the days
after 9/11 had the responsibility to see who

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:47.690
the heck else was out there.

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:52.190
And you track that by looking at all these
metadata collections.

00:35:52.190 --> 00:35:57.190
The second point you raised, though, which
is somewhat distinct, is a little bit related.

00:35:57.190 --> 00:36:02.550
This has to do with the fact that the U.S.
government, when it seeks to get information,

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:08.849
data, about, let's say, a foreigner, who's
really in a foreign country, in a data center

00:36:08.849 --> 00:36:13.070
that's in a foreign country, will go against
the service provider that's doing business

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:17.240
in the United States and say: Well, you have
to turn this over to us because we're going

00:36:17.240 --> 00:36:19.050
to subpoena you.

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:23.220
And that's an issue we used to confront with
banks.

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:29.420
And the question is, is that subpoena like
asking for bank records, which we typically

00:36:29.420 --> 00:36:31.300
allow the government to do?

00:36:31.300 --> 00:36:37.450
Or is it like telling a U.S. branch of a Swiss
bank: You have to break into the safety deposit

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:39.780
box of somebody in Switzerland?

00:36:39.780 --> 00:36:41.060
And we're not going to go to Swiss court.

00:36:41.060 --> 00:36:43.210
We're going to go to a U.S. court.

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:48.500
That highlights the problem of a world in
which our law is still sovereign centric but

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:49.930
the data moves around the globe.

00:36:49.930 --> 00:36:55.520
And the real answer is not to eliminate the
ability to get foreign evidence, but to find

00:36:55.520 --> 00:37:03.280
a cooperative and efficient way to get multiple
sovereigns to agree on a process to do this

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:07.270
quickly, but that respects the sovereignty
of the country, for example, whose citizen

00:37:07.270 --> 00:37:08.270
is involved.

00:37:08.270 --> 00:37:12.750
So again, this is not about closing that off,
but it is about avoiding what happened in

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:20.200
Brazil, where a judge shut down WhatsApp in
Brazil because the judge ordered, I guess

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:24.270
it was Facebook, to turn over data in the
U.S., it violated U.S. law.

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:27.670
And the company said, well, what do I do?

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:30.450
And that's not a good situation to be in for
anybody.

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:36.170
SEGAL: But I mean, the impact of the Snowden
revelations was more than the metadata, right?

00:37:36.170 --> 00:37:45.690
We have allegations that they undermined encryption,
that they hacked into Yahoo and Google servers,

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:51.089
that they were sitting on zero-days, that
they intercepted equipment and put implants

00:37:51.089 --> 00:37:52.089
in it.

00:37:52.089 --> 00:37:57.040
So I think the impact on the companies is
clearly much broader than the metadata.

00:37:57.040 --> 00:37:59.589
And that has never been repaired.

00:37:59.589 --> 00:38:01.900
VANCE: Right.

00:38:01.900 --> 00:38:08.940
And I think it's an important point that what
caused this stir was federal action.

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:09.940
(Laughter.)

00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:20.240
we, who are handling 95 percent of the criminal
cases in the country, are now trying to push

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:27.220
ahead to do our job when upset has been caused
that has nothing to do with us.

00:38:27.220 --> 00:38:30.869
And we only get access to devices through
search warrants.

00:38:30.869 --> 00:38:40.460
So again, obviously these are-they are overlapping
issues, but the Snowden revelations clearly

00:38:40.460 --> 00:38:44.230
have, I think, had a big impact on public
opinion.

00:38:44.230 --> 00:38:51.320
I understand clearly the relationship between
those revelations and the actions of the companies.

00:38:51.320 --> 00:38:59.490
But if-looking at Apple-if iOS 7, up to September
2014 was as secure as they said it was, and

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:03.650
if we don't have any indication that there's
ever been a breach, at least of the data at

00:39:03.650 --> 00:39:09.170
rest, by delivering a phone to them, what
is the problem Apple is trying to cure?

00:39:09.170 --> 00:39:13.109
Is it a public relations problem or is it
a privacy problem?

00:39:13.109 --> 00:39:17.310
And I think it may have been more a public
relations problem than a real privacy problem.

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:19.150
CROVITZ: Yes, ma'am.

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:20.980
Q: Thank you.

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:22.850
My name is Courtney Radsch.

00:39:22.850 --> 00:39:24.349
And a couple of questions.

00:39:24.349 --> 00:39:30.280
First, there were reports that when you gained-when
the FBI gained access to the phone, that the

00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:33.400
reason that they lost access to it is because
they changed the password.

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:37.310
So I'd like to know if that's true or not,
because then that actually seems to be more

00:39:37.310 --> 00:39:41.640
of an issue of governmental-you know, of failure
on the government's part, that they should

00:39:41.640 --> 00:39:43.730
deal with not the tech company.

00:39:43.730 --> 00:39:48.210
Also, given that we're at the Council on Foreign
Relations, I think it's a big ironic that

00:39:48.210 --> 00:39:52.400
we're talking about this as if it's only a
U.S. problem, because the fact is that journalists

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:55.090
and activists around the world depend on encryption.

00:39:55.090 --> 00:39:59.070
And so this does not happen in a United States
vacuum.

00:39:59.070 --> 00:40:03.210
And with respect to the local law enforcement,
I think one of the things we've seen is the

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:08.319
militarization of local law enforcement and
the transition of not only of military equipment

00:40:08.319 --> 00:40:11.500
but also intelligence equipment to local law
enforcement.

00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:17.980
And so this is, you know, within a broader
lack of trust in either local or national

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:18.980
governments.

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:24.540
And when you see law enforcement abuses ranging
from, you know, Black Lives Matter and Ferguson,

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:29.660
up through mass surveillance of U.S. citizens,
I think it's hard for us to trust the government

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:35.790
to do what's right, and that there's a difference
between communication that occurs when people

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:39.880
feel that they're under surveillance as when
they feel that they're able to communicate

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:44.950
in private, especially when you're the member
of a minority group, Muslim, a journalist

00:40:44.950 --> 00:40:49.589
and you have to think about source protection,
because that metadata can reveal the sources

00:40:49.589 --> 00:40:50.820
of journalists.

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:52.460
CROVITZ: Several questions there.

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:57.270
VANCE: Well, Mike-
CHERTOFF: No, you go first.

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:58.270
(Laughter.)

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:00.330
VANCE: Great questions.

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:02.349
And many questions.

00:41:02.349 --> 00:41:03.430
And no simple answers.

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:09.770
I think, first, what law enforcement needs
to do, and I think people in law enforcement

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:13.980
who are smart are, is to address these questions
directly.

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:17.890
I certainly try to do that, for example, on
the issue of race and criminal justice.

00:41:17.890 --> 00:41:23.140
I think these are big issues and prosecutors
and police can't-we have to explain what we're

00:41:23.140 --> 00:41:25.849
doing and why, and to answer some of your
questions.

00:41:25.849 --> 00:41:32.680
There's a technical question with regard to
whether we lost the opportunity in San Bernardino

00:41:32.680 --> 00:41:34.060
to access the phone.

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:36.020
Jim Comey has answered that question.

00:41:36.020 --> 00:41:39.010
If I try to repeat what he said I'm sure I'm
going to get it wrong.

00:41:39.010 --> 00:41:47.550
But I think there was-there was some apparent
confusion over getting access as a phone owned

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:53.310
by the county, and somehow that ended up having
the phone get locked up.

00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:55.329
So that's number one.

00:41:55.329 --> 00:42:02.150
And from the local law enforcement perspective,
I've never used a Stingray in our office,

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:05.819
which is the device that captures all sorts
of phones in the area.

00:42:05.819 --> 00:42:08.190
Our investigators don't use it.

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:12.490
If they did use them, they'd be getting a
search warrant for them.

00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:17.390
And you had a lot of other questions which
were just, it seems to me, based upon Americans

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:23.050
have lost trust-or, some Americans have lost
trust in law enforcement.

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:28.290
And I understand that.

00:42:28.290 --> 00:42:36.740
And it is why, whether the issue is race and
criminal justice or the issue is talking about

00:42:36.740 --> 00:42:43.050
how we need to adhere to constitutional standards
every time we are trying to get evidence through

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:48.349
court order, I think we have to do a better
job-not do a better job, but communicate about

00:42:48.349 --> 00:42:54.530
what we're doing, because we should get your
trust back, if we can.

00:42:54.530 --> 00:42:55.819
CROVITZ: Please.

00:42:55.819 --> 00:42:57.740
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:42:57.740 --> 00:42:59.030
Q: Hi.

00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:00.310
Thank you.

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:06.099
My name is Brooke (sp), from Congressman Hurd's
office.

00:43:06.099 --> 00:43:12.040
So I was wondering if you can comment on a
part of this debate which was sort of touched

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:15.200
on, and it has to do with the companies themselves.

00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:21.900
So the backdoor debate aside, I was wondering
what is the legal precedent for forcing a

00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:34.010
company to do something like create in every
case some sort of ability to open up encrypted

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:41.579
data, or, you know, if they can't because
they've created an encrypted phone that they

00:43:41.579 --> 00:43:47.800
don't have the, quote, unquote, "key" for,
to force them to create the key for it?

00:43:47.800 --> 00:43:50.359
And I had one more question, but I don't remember
it.

00:43:50.359 --> 00:43:51.359
(Laughter.)

00:43:51.359 --> 00:43:53.280
So I was wondering if you could comment on
that.

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:56.130
CHERTOFF: So I'll start on this one.

00:43:56.130 --> 00:43:58.560
So I think there are a couple of issues.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:05.050
There's a narrow issue in the case of San
Bernardino, which I guess has not been resolved

00:44:05.050 --> 00:44:09.720
because it's moot, about whether in the context
of a search warrant-I mean, the All Writs

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:14.660
Act, which allows-basically what the judge
does is says, you know, I'm granting this

00:44:14.660 --> 00:44:15.660
warrant.

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:21.470
And the authority to do anything-the company
has to do anything to effectuate the warrant

00:44:21.470 --> 00:44:22.530
that is necessary.

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:27.180
So that's-so, for example, if you're a landlord
in a house and there's a search warrant, you

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.480
have to, you know, open the door.

00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:33.470
The question that was raised, which has not
been resolved, is how far does that power

00:44:33.470 --> 00:44:34.470
go?

00:44:34.470 --> 00:44:40.730
Can you say to somebody: You have to write-spend
six months writing code to get around a problem?

00:44:40.730 --> 00:44:45.600
Well, that sounds a little bit like you're
enlisting them against their will in the police.

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:51.211
The broader question is whether you could
require as a congressional matter, or pass

00:44:51.211 --> 00:44:57.140
a law that would say you have to have the
capability with respect to any encrypted data

00:44:57.140 --> 00:45:00.290
for the company or the host to be able to
get into that data.

00:45:00.290 --> 00:45:03.950
I think, you know, if Congress passed that
law, it would probably be constitutional,

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:05.380
off the top of my head.

00:45:05.380 --> 00:45:09.040
I think it would be very unwise, because,
again, if you understand the way encryption

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:15.839
works-and again, I'm talking not so much about
device encryption here but about end-to-end

00:45:15.839 --> 00:45:18.170
encryption of data on a network.

00:45:18.170 --> 00:45:22.740
In order to create the ability to do that,
you would either have to have a duplicate

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:27.731
key in possession of the company, which would
then become a target for being stolen, or

00:45:27.731 --> 00:45:33.339
you'd have to build into the encryption a
flaw in the implementation, which is typically

00:45:33.339 --> 00:45:38.160
the way we tend to crack encryption now, because
the fact of the matter is even people who

00:45:38.160 --> 00:45:41.420
are trying to be perfect in encryption often
fail.

00:45:41.420 --> 00:45:44.580
For example, they'll use the same prime number
twice or something.

00:45:44.580 --> 00:45:48.670
And that radically reduces the amount of time
it takes to decrypt something.

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:53.380
So I think it's not so much a question in
the case of the broader issue of legal authority,

00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:59.400
as to the question of the tradeoff between
your ability to get general encryption protection

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:00.869
versus the individual case.

00:46:00.869 --> 00:46:05.920
And I know that in Congress now there's a
bill to have some kind of a digital commission

00:46:05.920 --> 00:46:11.440
to look at this, which probably-I don't know
that these issues are well-resolved in one-off

00:46:11.440 --> 00:46:12.440
cases.

00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:15.430
And I say this as a former judge.

00:46:15.430 --> 00:46:20.430
I don't know that judges are always the most
technologically astute and well-informed people.

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:21.430
(Laughter.)

00:46:21.430 --> 00:46:25.510
Getting a group to look at this, who have
the technical background, might be illuminating,

00:46:25.510 --> 00:46:26.510
actually.

00:46:26.510 --> 00:46:30.910
VANCE: OK, I'll jump in.

00:46:30.910 --> 00:46:32.710
CROVITZ: Please.

00:46:32.710 --> 00:46:40.670
VANCE: Companies historically in America have
been asked to-or required to do many things

00:46:40.670 --> 00:46:44.080
in order to address law enforcement imperatives.

00:46:44.080 --> 00:46:49.230
So banks didn't volunteer to have to file
currency transaction reports when more than

00:46:49.230 --> 00:46:51.350
$10,000 of cash is moved.

00:46:51.350 --> 00:46:54.859
They have to file them because we found out
that drug dealers were moving cash through

00:46:54.859 --> 00:46:55.859
banks.

00:46:55.859 --> 00:47:02.910
The phone companies didn't volunteer to have
their-to make their lines accessible to law

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:05.349
enforcement if they had Title 3 warrants.

00:47:05.349 --> 00:47:11.510
They had to, because everyone knew that bad
guys were using the phone to commit crimes

00:47:11.510 --> 00:47:13.530
and it was necessary to talk to them.

00:47:13.530 --> 00:47:18.829
So we aren't really, in my mind, in that different
a place today.

00:47:18.829 --> 00:47:24.910
We know criminals are using smartphone devices
for criminal purposes.

00:47:24.910 --> 00:47:29.440
There's a phone conversation-we actually have
a report from our office, which we'll make

00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:34.650
available to anybody who wants it outside-where
there's a conversation of an inmate in Rikers

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:37.430
Island who's talking with his confederate
outside.

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:44.099
And the inmate is asking the confederate:
Did you make sure you got the iOS 8 devices?

00:47:44.099 --> 00:47:47.400
Because the government can't get into them
and it's a gift from god.

00:47:47.400 --> 00:47:49.920
So the criminals have figured this out.

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:56.990
So the question is, is Apple really any different
than the banks, and all the money laundering

00:47:56.990 --> 00:48:00.780
regulations they have to follow and report
on and documents that they have to keep?

00:48:00.780 --> 00:48:04.319
Or is it that different from the phone companies
who have to provide access that require them

00:48:04.319 --> 00:48:12.180
to set up a connect-something probably requiring
software development as well as a physical

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:13.180
location?

00:48:13.180 --> 00:48:15.010
And I think the answer is, not really.

00:48:15.010 --> 00:48:20.180
SEGAL: I think that there is a difference
in the sense, to the earlier question about

00:48:20.180 --> 00:48:25.910
our other foreign policy goals of protecting
dissidents and journalists and others in other

00:48:25.910 --> 00:48:26.910
places.

00:48:26.910 --> 00:48:30.980
So we're going to have a situation where you
can imagine, where the companies are going

00:48:30.980 --> 00:48:35.260
to be forced by numerous governments to do
this issue.

00:48:35.260 --> 00:48:39.109
And then the question is, who are they going
to say no to, right?

00:48:39.109 --> 00:48:42.330
So are they going to say, well, we've agreed
to do this for the United States.

00:48:42.330 --> 00:48:43.630
We've agreed to do it for India.

00:48:43.630 --> 00:48:45.730
We'll do it for Germany.

00:48:45.730 --> 00:48:47.940
We're not going to do it for China.

00:48:47.940 --> 00:48:51.960
Maybe we'll do it-you know, so then I think
the situation becomes slightly different.

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:58.880
Financial sector, we've had agreement basically-it's
been able-we've been more able to shape norms

00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:02.819
on antiterrorism and criminal-kind of a shard
interest there.

00:49:02.819 --> 00:49:06.650
On the free speech issue, we're going to face
a fundamental division that we're not going

00:49:06.650 --> 00:49:07.800
to be able to resolve.

00:49:07.800 --> 00:49:09.240
VANCE: Let's test that.

00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:10.930
Let's look at China.

00:49:10.930 --> 00:49:17.550
Let's look at the request China made to Apple
for information on Apple accounts before iOS

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:19.869
8 and after.

00:49:19.869 --> 00:49:30.130
So before iOS 8, between July and December
of 2014, China made 31 requests to Apple for

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:32.660
account data out of 39 accounts.

00:49:32.660 --> 00:49:39.040
And Apple provided in response to the Chinese
request 32 percent of the time, OK?

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:44.970
Between January and June of 2015, the next
six months, now this is after iOS 8 has come

00:49:44.970 --> 00:49:52.400
in, China made 24 requests for 85 accounts,
and Apple responded to 29 percent of those

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:53.400
requests.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:57.410
And these are-Apple describes this as the
equivalent of a search warrant.

00:49:57.410 --> 00:50:05.690
And then July to December 2015, 32 requests
out many thousands of accounts which I think

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:12.040
relates to a phishing investigation-there
was a lot of phishing going on-P-F-fishing.

00:50:12.040 --> 00:50:14.000
And so Apple responded to 53 percent.

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:23.089
So my point is that we have data from Apple
that suggests that when Apple went to full-disk

00:50:23.089 --> 00:50:31.089
encryption, it really wasn't that different
from when Apple had access-when Apple had

00:50:31.089 --> 00:50:34.650
access through iOS 7 in response to government
search warrants.

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:38.070
So my point is that this was happening before.

00:50:38.070 --> 00:50:39.980
It's happened after the switchover.

00:50:39.980 --> 00:50:50.420
And so when we are told that the world's going
to end, and that if China's going to use this

00:50:50.420 --> 00:50:55.630
power thousands of times, I'm not sure the
data supports it from Apple.

00:50:55.630 --> 00:51:03.349
CHERTOFF: Yeah, I'm not sure-there's a bit
of a difference, just to be clear, about the

00:51:03.349 --> 00:51:06.619
issue that was presented in the Apple case,
which was getting onto the device, versus

00:51:06.619 --> 00:51:09.030
the data which had been uploaded to the cloud.

00:51:09.030 --> 00:51:15.130
My recollection from the press reporting in
San Bernardino was actually any of the data

00:51:15.130 --> 00:51:17.970
that was backed up to the cloud was furnished
to the FBI.

00:51:17.970 --> 00:51:22.670
And the reason they had a problem at the end
was that somebody by altering the password

00:51:22.670 --> 00:51:25.079
stopped the automatic backup system.

00:51:25.079 --> 00:51:30.470
So it's not been the case, as I understand
it, that the company says: If we have unencrypted

00:51:30.470 --> 00:51:32.260
data we will refuse to turn it over.

00:51:32.260 --> 00:51:34.240
I think they agree they have to.

00:51:34.240 --> 00:51:40.130
It's do they need to forebear from having
a system which they don't have access to because

00:51:40.130 --> 00:51:43.210
if they don't have access to it they will
not be able to comply.

00:51:43.210 --> 00:51:45.520
And that's really what the issue is about.

00:51:45.520 --> 00:51:48.099
SEGAL: And all Apple user data has to be stored
in China.

00:51:48.099 --> 00:51:50.660
So they may have just simply turned over the
data that they had.

00:51:50.660 --> 00:51:51.660
CHERTOFF: Right.

00:51:51.660 --> 00:51:52.660
Yeah.

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:53.660
VANCE: Does it have to be stored in China?

00:51:53.660 --> 00:51:54.660
Or is that an accommodation?

00:51:54.660 --> 00:51:57.911
CHERTOFF: Well, I think that's the condition
as Chinese data.

00:51:57.911 --> 00:52:02.140
And that gets us to the point that Alan raised
earlier, which is you have to reconcile the

00:52:02.140 --> 00:52:08.609
fact that you have multiple sovereigns and
you have global data.

00:52:08.609 --> 00:52:14.359
So it would be one thing if- and maybe a condition
of entry into a particular marketplace, you

00:52:14.359 --> 00:52:19.280
can debate whether you should obey it or not,
is that the data generated in that particular

00:52:19.280 --> 00:52:20.960
country needs to be held there.

00:52:20.960 --> 00:52:25.690
It would be quite something else if the Chinese
requested data of an American held in the

00:52:25.690 --> 00:52:29.089
U.S., and that were turned over without appropriate
process.

00:52:29.089 --> 00:52:34.319
And that gets us exactly to the issue Alan
raised about how do you reconcile these conflicting

00:52:34.319 --> 00:52:37.530
jurisdictional issues.

00:52:37.530 --> 00:52:42.560
CROVITZ: Please.

00:52:42.560 --> 00:52:47.400
Q: Henry Farrell at George Washington University.

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:52.900
Just building on all of that, does U.S. law
enforcement pay attention to these questions

00:52:52.900 --> 00:52:58.490
that Adam raised about possible demonstration
effects, setting precedents, creating weaknesses

00:52:58.490 --> 00:53:07.079
that might be exploited by less sort of careful
actors in the world than the U.S.?

00:53:07.079 --> 00:53:11.700
And should it pay attention to these questions,
or is that outside of its mandate?

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:17.480
VANCE: Well, I think, from my perspective,
the questions are very big ones.

00:53:17.480 --> 00:53:27.710
And what I have found-what I disagree with
is this point, and its following on your question:

00:53:27.710 --> 00:53:34.500
Other companies historically have had to adjust
when it was clear that the product they were

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:45.059
selling and using was being used by criminals
and it was a real problem.

00:53:45.059 --> 00:53:51.840
Apple's response, for a variety of reasons,
was to essentially cut off access to the data

00:53:51.840 --> 00:53:54.520
in the devices themselves.

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:59.160
And it did so using its own-it made its decision.

00:53:59.160 --> 00:54:03.300
The consequences of that, however, are broad.

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:07.670
And they will touch all jurisdictions of the
United States.

00:54:07.670 --> 00:54:11.790
And they will impact the victims in cases
all over the United States.

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:18.720
And when something that big happens, my question
is: Who gets to decide where the line should

00:54:18.720 --> 00:54:22.570
be drawn between privacy and public safety?

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:28.089
Should it be two companies whose operating
systems run 96.7 percent of the world's smartphone

00:54:28.089 --> 00:54:29.089
market?

00:54:29.089 --> 00:54:30.970
I'm not worried about Apple running out of
money.

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:33.900
I'm not worried about Apple losing market
share.

00:54:33.900 --> 00:54:36.150
They own it.

00:54:36.150 --> 00:54:40.359
These are two phenomenally wealthy companies,
and they decided we're going to draw the line

00:54:40.359 --> 00:54:42.819
of public safety and privacy over here.

00:54:42.819 --> 00:54:45.290
I really don't think they get to do that.

00:54:45.290 --> 00:54:47.950
I think Congress gets to do that.

00:54:47.950 --> 00:54:53.430
And that's why I believe there should be a
political and legislative discussion about

00:54:53.430 --> 00:54:55.420
this, probably.

00:54:55.420 --> 00:55:00.620
Our cases in court have to move according
to timetables set by statute.

00:55:00.620 --> 00:55:04.460
Statute of limitations are starting to run-can
run on cases that are very important.

00:55:04.460 --> 00:55:09.240
We don't have the luxury, particularly at
state court, of tons of time.

00:55:09.240 --> 00:55:15.799
And that's the reality of how this is playing
out in our state courts.

00:55:15.799 --> 00:55:22.440
And it's a reality that if you're a victim,
it impacts you.

00:55:22.440 --> 00:55:27.130
CROVITZ: So I'm going to take the privilege
of the presider by asking the last question

00:55:27.130 --> 00:55:33.599
of the evening, which is, you know, the Senate
Intelligence Committee, in a bipartisan manner,

00:55:33.599 --> 00:55:39.150
introduced a draft bill that would require
technology companies essentially to make-to

00:55:39.150 --> 00:55:45.000
be able to respond to requests for information,
much like phone companies, telecom companies

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:47.760
have had to for many years.

00:55:47.760 --> 00:55:53.200
And in that bill, at the beginning, it says
no entity is above the law, reflecting some

00:55:53.200 --> 00:55:56.410
of the views that Cy Vance has made here.

00:55:56.410 --> 00:56:02.369
My question is, there's also legislation in
France and in Britain.

00:56:02.369 --> 00:56:07.380
Of the three countries-France, Britain, United
States-which one is likeliest to pass the

00:56:07.380 --> 00:56:09.099
law first?

00:56:09.099 --> 00:56:10.630
(Laughter.)

00:56:10.630 --> 00:56:12.869
Or maybe I should say last.

00:56:12.869 --> 00:56:18.830
VANCE: Well, I think they're likely to pass
it before we do.

00:56:18.830 --> 00:56:23.849
The presidents of each of those countries
have been very outspoken in their-in their

00:56:23.849 --> 00:56:27.160
need to take on this issue of encryption.

00:56:27.160 --> 00:56:28.680
I don't know when it will happen.

00:56:28.680 --> 00:56:33.960
But I think it actually may help America sort
itself out in this process, because if the

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:41.710
French and the British create laws which address
this issue of what can and cannot be encrypted,

00:56:41.710 --> 00:56:47.859
that I think will have an impact on American
legislators, and I think may give them, I

00:56:47.859 --> 00:56:53.400
hope, comfort that they can pass legislation
without fear that the world's going end.

00:56:53.400 --> 00:56:58.440
CHERTOFF: So, Gordon, let me ask this question,
because this is, as I said earlier, not just

00:56:58.440 --> 00:57:00.790
about getting to a particular phone.

00:57:00.790 --> 00:57:03.869
If you passed a bill like that, what would
happen to WhatsApp?

00:57:03.869 --> 00:57:05.460
You know, disappearing messages.

00:57:05.460 --> 00:57:09.460
That would become illegal, because if the
message isn't stored, if it disappears as

00:57:09.460 --> 00:57:11.520
soon as you read it, you can't produce it.

00:57:11.520 --> 00:57:17.089
So now what you're really doing is you're
basically making it illegal almost to destroy

00:57:17.089 --> 00:57:19.020
any communication.

00:57:19.020 --> 00:57:21.460
Maybe it should be impossible to delete your
email.

00:57:21.460 --> 00:57:24.190
Maybe there should be a rule that you can
never shred a piece of paper.

00:57:24.190 --> 00:57:28.711
I mean, we don't generally operate in this
country on the assumption that you have to

00:57:28.711 --> 00:57:35.079
organize your life so that in the event somebody
wants to subpoena you or investigate you to

00:57:35.079 --> 00:57:36.079
make it easy.

00:57:36.079 --> 00:57:37.839
In fact, I can make it easier still.

00:57:37.839 --> 00:57:42.040
Let's have a law that says this has to be
on all the time, has to record everything

00:57:42.040 --> 00:57:45.030
that I say and video and audio.

00:57:45.030 --> 00:57:47.040
And then if I do something wrong it'll be
really easy.

00:57:47.040 --> 00:57:51.079
It would have made my job very much easier
when I was a prosecutor.

00:57:51.079 --> 00:57:55.280
I think, you know, the phone, to me, is a
small subset of a larger issue.

00:57:55.280 --> 00:58:02.210
And that's why I said earlier I do think-I
agree with Cy, it is worthy of study.

00:58:02.210 --> 00:58:06.730
It shouldn't be decided by the happenstance
of a particular case.

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:10.690
But I do think we need to understand that
there's a broad implication of this beyond

00:58:10.690 --> 00:58:13.810
just the particular configuration of a particular
phone.

00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:17.859
VANCE: And I think there are actually ways
to go forward on this.

00:58:17.859 --> 00:58:22.330
Right now we have two sides who really are
on opposites-polar opposites.

00:58:22.330 --> 00:58:25.940
And there isn't-there really doesn't seem
to be a public discussion about how can we

00:58:25.940 --> 00:58:27.400
move forward?

00:58:27.400 --> 00:58:30.180
Imagine a world in which there were different
levels of encryption.

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:34.730
There might be one level of encryption for
communications and content, and another level

00:58:34.730 --> 00:58:39.900
of encryption when it dealt with autonomous
moving vehicles that-self-driving vehicles.

00:58:39.900 --> 00:58:48.950
That second group is something that obviously-if
that's captured, that's immediately dangerous.

00:58:48.950 --> 00:58:52.840
By content and communications may be different.

00:58:52.840 --> 00:59:01.860
There's a-we should really sit down and we
should explore whether it is in fact true-and

00:59:01.860 --> 00:59:07.140
I think part of the problem is that Apple
in particular has said hell no, no way, no

00:59:07.140 --> 00:59:08.299
time, no how.

00:59:08.299 --> 00:59:16.220
And I don't think that's helpful to anybody
in resolving this, although I respect their

00:59:16.220 --> 00:59:19.210
ingenuity and the passion of their beliefs.

00:59:19.210 --> 00:59:23.089
It's not helping us figure this out.

00:59:23.089 --> 00:59:27.810
SEGAL: I suspect we won't have the luxury
of having that rational debate.

00:59:27.810 --> 00:59:32.700
I don't think we can discount the role that
fear has played in all of these discussions.

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:38.420
And so you can imagine, you know, an event
that happens here or in France or in Britain

00:59:38.420 --> 00:59:39.990
that would totally shift the debate.

00:59:39.990 --> 00:59:45.210
And people will rush to make a law so this
won't happen again, even though the law will

00:59:45.210 --> 00:59:46.210
not be effective.

00:59:46.210 --> 00:59:51.710
VANCE: And if that happens, that law is going
to look a lot worse for Apple and Google than

00:59:51.710 --> 00:59:56.390
the law that we could perhaps move forward
to if we sat at the table and sorted this

00:59:56.390 --> 00:59:57.390
out.

00:59:57.390 --> 01:00:00.430
CROVITZ: And in the meantime, the Council
on Foreign Relations, always trying to do

01:00:00.430 --> 01:00:09.251
its bit for the world, Mike Chertoff has dictated
a Chinese statute requiring Chinese statute

01:00:09.251 --> 01:00:10.809
requiring Chinese citizens to keep their phones
on.

01:00:10.809 --> 01:00:12.480
It's a fantastic idea.

01:00:12.480 --> 01:00:13.480
(Laughter.)

01:00:13.480 --> 01:00:14.800
So please thank the panel in the customary
way.

01:00:14.800 --> 01:00:15.800
(Applause.)

01:00:15.800 --> 01:00:15.801
(END)

